The ternary operator is a compact way to write a simple if–else condition in a single line of code.

🔹 Basic idea

It evaluates a condition and returns one of two values:

one if the condition is true
another if the condition is false
🔹 Syntax
condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false;
🔹 Example (JavaScript)
let age = 18;
let result = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
console.log(result); // Adult
🔹 Equivalent if–else
let result;
if (age >= 18) {
  result = "Adult";
} else {
  result = "Minor";
}
🔹 Why use it?
Makes code shorter and cleaner for simple conditions
Useful for assignments and return statements

Hoisting is a behavior in languages like JavaScript where variable and function declarations are moved to the top of their scope during compilation, before the code actually runs.

🔹 What does that mean?

It means you can sometimes use a variable or function before it is declared in your code.

🔹 Example (Variables with var)
console.log(x); // undefined
var x = 5;
What actually happens internally:
var x;
console.log(x); // undefined
x = 5;

👉 The declaration is hoisted, but the assignment stays in place.

🔹 Example (Functions)
greet(); // Works!

function greet() {
  console.log("Hello!");
}

👉 Entire function declarations are hoisted, so you can call them before defining them.

🔹 Important differences
1. var (hoisted, initialized as undefined)
console.log(a); // undefined
var a = 10;
2. let and const (hoisted but not initialized)
console.log(b); // ❌ ReferenceError
let b = 10;

